## Summary (from the raw lines above)

- Runs: 10; all exited 0; every run reported `finalVersion: 40` and
  `committedSubmissions: 40`.
- p95 samples (ms): 1037.167, 1037.492, 1057.668, 1063.085, 1082.760,
  1094.394, 1099.940, 1103.536, 1108.321, 1109.063.
- p95 min = 1037.167 ms, max = 1109.063 ms, mean = 1079.343 ms,
  spread = 71.896 ms.
- Largest observed p95 is 390.937 ms below 1500 ms (26.1% headroom);
  smallest observed p95 is 462.833 ms below 1500 ms (30.9% headroom).
- These values are above the earlier single-run p95 values (1009.037 ms in
  02-pass.log and 957.856 ms in 03-mutation.log). p50 in this repeat run is
  488.135–574.867 ms and max is 1098.098–1185.267 ms. The spread is consistent
  with CAS retry contention: tasks that lose the version race retry, so p95
  depends on scheduling and on how many retries occur, not only on the fixed
  simulated store delay.
- This is an in-process harness, not a server or network measurement. The
  result is machine- and load-dependent; it is evidence for this machine at
  this time, not a production p95.

## Decision on the assertion

Keep `p95 <= 1500` as a hard assertion for this dedicated load harness. NFR1
requires a measured budget, all 10 runs passed with at least 390.937 ms of
headroom, and this harness is not part of the default unit suite. It is a
harness budget, not a production SLO. If a slower/noisier CI machine fails it,
record that environment and the observed p95 rather than silently weakening the
threshold; moving it to a report-only check would require an explicit decision
and a documented replacement acceptance criterion.
